// CLOSED 2026-09-23 (squeue #313): a reference pair over raw/fn pointers compares THROUGH the references (the pointers are Copy scalars).
// SOUNDNESS QUEUE row refptr_param_eq_compares_outer_ref (tier 1, `run 1`).
// LEGAL PROGRAM, COMPILES CLEAN, COMPUTES THE WRONG ANSWER: `==` on a `&*mut T`
// PARAMETER compares the OUTER REFERENCE instead of the raw pointer it points to.
//
//   fn same(x: &*mut i64, y: &*mut i64) -> bool { x == y }
//
// `p` and `q` are two copies of ONE pointer held in two distinct locals, so `&p` and
// `&q` are different addresses while `*(&p) == *(&q)` is true. logosc compiles this
// clean and the binary exits 1 (the `false` arm). rustc exits 0.
//
// rustc 1.98.1 --edition 2024 MEASURED 2026-09-17: COMPILES and RUNS exit 0.
// Twin: src/compiler/probes/2026-09-17b-ptrmentionland/rust/D02_refptr_no_inner_ref_control.rs
//
// ⚠ CONTROL, SAME BINARY: the DIRECT comparison `p == q` with no `&*mut` parameter in
// sight runs 0 — correctly (D01_direct_ptr_eq_control). So the defect is the reference
// LAYER over the pointer at a parameter, not raw-pointer equality.
//
// ⚠ THIS ROW CORRECTS A RECORDED CLAIM. Round 2026-09-17a found this wrong answer only
// through `&*mut &'a i64` (an INNER reference under the pointer), where the shape is
// refused on base by an unrelated region defect, and concluded the miscompile was
// "unreachable until this round's region door opens — doors in series". MEASURED here:
// with NO inner reference the very same wrong answer is reachable on the SHIPPED
// compiler today, with no arm installed (build_hash 8f5483d04b7c49ac 43). The row
// `refptr_inner_region_elision_demands_static_refused` is the region-door spelling of
// the same shape and stays as it is; this row is the defect itself, in its minimal form.
//
// FIX = exit 0, and the direct/parameter spellings agree.
package refptr_param_eq_compares_outer_ref;
fn same(x: &*mut i64, y: &*mut i64) -> bool {
    return x == y;
}
fn main() -> i32 {
    let mut a: i64 = 1i64;
    let p: *mut i64 = &mut a;
    let q: *mut i64 = p;
    if same(&p, &q) { return 0i32; }
    return 1i32;
}
